WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.600
You extended GTM OS by
definition, never by fork.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.100
Now put it on a server your team
can reach and nobody else can.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.933
One command brings it up, and a
reverse proxy is the only door.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.667
By the end you'll have your instance
on your own domain over HTTPS,

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.833
with every application port closed.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.267
Leo opens the console and API
ports so the team can get in.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:22.233
That is how self hosted tools
end up on a scanner's list.

00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:26.567
Reachable by the team is a different
goal from reachable by everyone.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.900
Step one, keep the ports local.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.067
On a server, gtm up keeps the ports on
the local machine, like the database.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:38.633
Local means 127.0.0.1. That covers
the console, the API and MCP.

00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:43.033
Nothing on the network reaches them
directly, and that's deliberate.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:46.600
The address is one setting:
GTMOS_BIND_ADDRESS.

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.400
To change it on purpose, set it
in .env and run gtm up again.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.933
Or pass the bind flag when
you create the instance.

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.033
A private address serves
a proxy on another host.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:01.233
And 0.0.0.0 opens every interface,
so keep that behind a firewall.

00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:02.767
The .env value is kept.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:06.433
A flag on an existing instance
is reported and ignored.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:07.833
On an older instance,

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.533
the first gtm up or gtm upgrade
warns you before it changes anything:

00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:16.533
these ports become local only.
It adds the setting to your .env.

00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:21.033
If people reach it by address
and port, put a proxy in front.

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:22.633
Or set 0.0.0.0 on purpose.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.600
Step two, the domain. Point one
parent domain at the server.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.800
Give it one host name for the
console and one for the API.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.500
Add one for MCP if outside agents need it.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.033
Open only ports 80 and
443 in the firewall.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.033
Step three, tell GTM OS where it lives.

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:42.367
In .env, set the three public
addresses: PUBLIC_API_URL,

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:48.233
PUBLIC_CONSOLE_URL and PUBLIC_MCP_URL.
Use https, with no trailing slash.

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:52.633
For subdomains of one parent,
also set GTMOS_COOKIE_DOMAIN,

00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:57.800
or the CRM screens fail to load. Then
run gtm up. No rebuild is needed.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:01.367
Step four, the proxy. Caddy is
the shortest route to HTTPS.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:04.933
Write one block per host name
that forwards to the local port.

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:09.000
Caddy gets and renews the certificates
for you. Then run caddy reload.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.900
The ports in the file are
the host ports from .env,

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.867
so use yours if you changed them.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.867
nginx works too. Get certificates
first, for example with certbot.

00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:21.867
Then add a server block per host that
forwards to the same local ports.

00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:26.100
Pass the host and forwarded protocol
headers, allow upgrades on the console,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.300
and redirect port 80 to HTTPS.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.500
Step five, check from
outside and from inside.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.000
The API health address should answer ok.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.433
The console should return a
page or a redirect to login.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:40.200
A socket listing should
show only 127.0.0.1

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.933
for the application
ports and the database.

00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:45.800
Seeing 0.0.0.0 means a port is exposed.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.700
Fix the bind address and run gtm up again.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.900
Or ask your agent: "Is this instance
ready, and what is missing".

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.700
Pro move: put that socket
listing in your deploy checklist.

00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:58.200
Last, keep it safe.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.233
gtm up generates the secrets
for you: the database password,

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:05.267
the auth secret, the master
key and the audit signing key,

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:08.767
all written into .env. Keep that
file out of version control,

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.800
and readable only by the service account.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.233
The master key seals
every stored credential.

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:15.500
And GTMOS_LIVE must be true or false,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.467
because production images
refuse to start without it.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.633
Back up on a schedule.

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:22.800
The script dumps every organization,
but not the master key.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.000
Without it, every stored connection
and token is lost on restore.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:27.900
So escrow the key separately.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.100
The dump isn't encrypted,
so restrict the folder.

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:32.433
The updater is optional.

00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:35.367
gtm upgrade does the whole
job from the command line.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.767
Install the updater
with gtm updater install

00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:40.033
only if you want the Update now button.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.667
Someone on your team will say: a
proxy is one more thing to run.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:47.967
It is, and it's the only thing
facing the internet. It ends HTTPS.

00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:50.200
Everything behind it sits on local ports.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.333
So a firewall mistake doesn't
expose the application.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.400
One boundary. This clip covers
one server, with Docker.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.667
A client's own cloud isn't covered,
because the live apply isn't verified.

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.233
Google Cloud and Azure
aren't supported yet.

00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:07.133
Say it back: Keep the ports local,
point a domain at one proxy,

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:10.033
set the public addresses,
and check from both sides.

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:11.800
Then escrow the master key.

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:14.600
Your instance now sits on
your own domain over HTTPS.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.067
It's closed to everything but the
proxy, with secrets and backups handled.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:20.133
The next clip is Upgrading:

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:23.333
moving to a new release
without touching your data.
